# The World's Urban Districts With the Most Skyscrapers: A Building-by-Building Tally (≥150 m)

## TL;DR
- **Manhattan (New York City) is the single administrative district with the most ≥150 m skyscrapers on Earth — roughly 291 of NYC's 324 — but this reflects concentration in one borough; China's Pearl River Delta districts (Futian and Nanshan in Shenzhen, Tianhe in Guangzhou) and Hong Kong's Sai Kung (LOHAS Park) each hold ~100+ and are the only rivals.**
- **The global ranking is distorted by how CTBUH counts towers: multi-tower residential mega-estates (LOHAS Park in Hong Kong, Dubai Marina, Pudong's Lujiazui) inflate district counts because each identical tower is counted separately — so a "district with most skyscrapers" list is really a list of the densest tower clusters.**
- **≥200 m counts are far more reliable than ≥150 m counts; Shenzhen's Nanshan (61 buildings over 200 m per Skyscraper Atlas) and Futian, plus Manhattan (~95 of NYC's 102), lead that stricter metric. Numbers below are exact at city level (CTBUH) but mostly estimated at district level, since no per-district tabulation existed before this compilation.**

## Key Findings

Skyscraper counts (≥150 m completed/topped-out, architectural height) are authoritative at the **city** level via CTBUH / The Skyscraper Center, but **no comprehensive per-district tabulation existed for any of the major skyline cities** before this compilation. Building this required assigning individual towers to districts using coordinates, addresses, and cluster knowledge. The reliability of each district figure is stated explicitly.

The most important structural finding: **CTBUH counts each tower individually**, so districts dominated by repetitive residential estates (each estate = 5–50 near-identical towers) rank far higher than prestige CBDs of famous landmarks. Hong Kong is #1 in the world for ≥150 m buildings almost entirely because of residential estate clusters, not its iconic Central skyline.

A second finding worth flagging up front: **the source databases disagree materially**, and the gaps are large. For Hong Kong alone, Wikipedia (citing CTBUH) gives 567–569 buildings ≥150 m, while Guinness World Records (also citing CTBUH, as of 30 January 2025) gives **669** — a ~100-building discrepancy in a single city. Seoul is 86 (CTBUH) vs 126 (Wikipedia); Metro Manila ~129 (CVU component-city sum) vs 215 (Wikipedia); Bangkok 129 (in the CTBUH database) vs 204 (Wikipedia total). Any definitive list must pick one authority and apply it consistently; this compilation uses CTBUH/The Skyscraper Center as the spine and notes deviations.

