## Recommendations

**For readers wanting the defensible headline:** Report **two rankings, not one.** (1) *By administrative district:* Manhattan is #1 by a wide margin (~291), and the CBD-anchored districts (Futian, Nanshan, Tianhe, Pudong, Yuzhong, the Loop, Minato) are the meaningful CBD leaders. (2) *By tower cluster including residential estates:* Hong Kong's Sai Kung and Dubai Marina jump up because of estate repetition. State which you mean, every time.

**Staged next steps to reach true exactness (the counts that remain estimates):**
1. *First priority — the four districts that decide the #2–#5 global ranks:* enumerate every ≥150 m tower in Shenzhen's **Futian and Nanshan**, Guangzhou's **Tianhe**, and Shanghai's **Pudong** from The Skyscraper Center, splitting queries by height band, completion year, or function to beat the 25-result cap. These four all sit in the ~90–120 range and their exact order is currently unresolved.
2. *Second — Hong Kong Sai Kung and Yau Tsim Mong:* verify each LOHAS Park and Union Square tower against the 150 m line (many residential towers sit right at the threshold), which will settle whether Sai Kung outranks the Shenzhen districts. Also reconcile the 569-vs-669 Wikipedia/Guinness gap, since it could shift several Hong Kong districts upward by 10–20 each.
3. *Third — finish the un-completed cities:* Toronto (Old Toronto), Moscow (Presnensky), Sydney (CBD + Parramatta), and the extras (Singapore URA planning areas, Jakarta's 5 administrative cities, Osaka wards, Istanbul, Busan/Haeundae-gu).
4. *Resolve database discrepancies:* Seoul (126 Wikipedia vs 86 CTBUH), Metro Manila (215 Wikipedia vs ~129 CVU component-city sum), Bangkok (204 vs 129 in CTBUH DB), Hong Kong (569 vs 669). Decide one authority (recommend CTBUH/CVU as the consistent spine) and apply it citywide before publishing.

**Benchmarks that would change the ranking:** If Futian or Nanshan verifies above ~145, either could challenge for the #2 spot behind Manhattan. If Manhattan's Midtown is treated as its own sub-district, it alone (~190–200) still outranks every non-Manhattan district worldwide. If a "distinct development" count (not per-tower) is adopted, Hong Kong's Sai Kung and Dubai Marina collapse out of the top ranks and prestige CBDs (Manhattan, Futian, Tianhe, the Loop) rise.

## Caveats
- **District figures are mostly estimates.** Only the city totals (CTBUH), NYC boroughs, Jersey City, London boroughs, Melbourne precincts, and Shenzhen Nanshan's ≥200 m count (61) are firm. Everything labeled Est/P should be treated as ±20–40%. This is a first-generation compilation intended to be refined tower-by-tower, exactly as the task envisioned.
- **Counting methodology dominates results.** CTBUH counts each tower separately, so residential mega-estates inflate district counts. This is the single biggest caveat for any "most skyscrapers" claim and the reason Hong Kong leads the world.
- **Database disagreements are large and unresolved:** Hong Kong 567–569 (Wikipedia) vs 669 (Guinness, both citing CTBUH); Seoul 86 vs 126; Metro Manila ~129 vs 215; Bangkok 129 vs 204; Shenzhen 443 (older Wikipedia prose) vs 469 (2025 CTBUH). Wikipedia generally counts more (includes non-CTBUH towers); CTBUH/CVU is stricter and more consistent.
- **Boundary-ambiguous buildings:** Cullinan West (Sham Shui Po / Yau Tsim Mong border), Primrose Hill (Tsuen Wan / Kwai Tsing), and several Bangkok riverside towers straddling Khlong San / Bang Rak could shift a handful of counts.
- **Not completed this pass:** Singapore, Jakarta, Osaka, Istanbul, and Busan beyond noting Haeundae-gu's dominance. Toronto, Moscow, and Sydney have city totals but only estimated (and not re-verified) district splits — the search budget was reached before these could be built up building-by-building.
- **"Topped-out" vs "completed":** counts include topped-out buildings where CTBUH does; a stricter completed-only count would be marginally lower in fast-building cities (Shenzhen, Wuhan, Dubai, Mumbai).
- **Sources:** The Skyscraper Center / CTBUH (city totals and building coordinates); Wikipedia "List of tallest buildings in [city]" pages (per-city and per-borough tables, coordinate data); Skyscraper Atlas (Shenzhen district ≥200 m counts); SkyscraperCity "London 100m+ Tower Index" (London borough tags); Grokipedia/CTBUH (Chongqing district ≥200 m counts); Guinness World Records (Hong Kong 669 figure); SKYDB (Chengdu). District assignments for Hong Kong were reconstructed from coordinate-tagged Wikipedia data — the first such tabulation known to exist.